  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The Dodgers broke it open in the eighth, and that's when the Giants' fragile hold on this game fell apart entirely.",
    "what_this_means": "Cesar Perdomo gave them a clean four-plus innings and only two runs. Exactly what you need in September baseball. Jung Hoo Lee drove in a run with his homer, and the offense scraped together just enough to stay within arm's reach through six. But the eighth inning buried them. Philip Abner couldn't hold the bridge. Five runs scored. The game that was still there became the game that wasn't. They had the window. Runners on base, chances to extend it. And didn't find the exit. Fourteen strikeouts is not a margin you can survive. Two runs left everything on the field.",
    "what_to_watch": "Tarik Skubal takes the ball tonight for Los Angeles, carrying a 2.70 ERA into a game the Giants cannot afford to let slip the same way. Same ballpark, same division rival, one chance to reset before it gets worse.",
